// expect-exit: 0
// mem-cap-mib: 64
//
// `bytes_copy_string` — THE MANAGED HALF, EMPTY.
//
// WAS -- COMMITTED RED AND PENDING, from the second cycle 0.0 audit triage to
// cycle 0.0.4b. At compiler `3d15ac9`, `bytes_copy_string` on an EMPTY `Bytes`
// leaked 32.2 bytes per call, so under the 64 MiB cap this file exited **92,
// `HeapOom`**, where its non-empty sibling exits 0. The leak was a **compiler
// defect — DEF-25**, raised under W-11 rather than worked around, confirmed by
// the compiler side and fixed at `fe42dba`, which that pin did not contain.
// Their words: *nothing in library code needs a guard.* So the file carried
// `pending-until: fe42dba`: it was built, linked, RUN, its actual exit printed,
// and counted as neither a pass nor a failure (RX-146).
//
// NOW -- PASSING, AND AN ORDINARY MEMBER OF THE RUN'S DENOMINATOR. `c3bdae2` is
// the first pin this repository has taken that contains `fe42dba` (`git
// merge-base --is-ancestor fe42dba c3bdae2` exits 0; against `3d15ac9` it exits
// 1). At that re-pin this file exited 0 under its cap, the harness reddened the
// run with "IS NOW STALE", and the marker was deleted (cycle 0.0.4b). THAT IS
// ALL THAT HAPPENED: the file started passing, the harness said so, the marker
// went. It is not evidence about the marker mechanism in general -- whether it
// reads its named commit, what else it can absorb -- which is the third cycle
// 0.0 audit's BL-6, and the cycle close's to answer.
//
// What this header said while the file was red, kept because it is why the fix
// was the compiler's and not a guard here:
//
// **DO NOT MAKE THIS PASS.** The three ways to do so are all wrong and all
// available: lower the count, raise the cap, or put
// `if (b.len == 0i64) { pass ""; }` in `bytes_copy_string`. The third is the
// tempting one and it is the one `CLAUDE.md` forbids by name — a workaround
// buried in library code outlives the bug, and this one would sit in the
// hottest accessor of the byte sink every replacement in this library is
// composed into, long after the compiler stopped needing it.
//
// WHY THE EMPTY PATH IS THE ORDINARY CASE AND NOT AN EDGE. Cycle 0.1's parser
// and 0.6's replacement are this function's first users, and "the result is
// empty" is what a replacement that deletes produces, and what a capture that
// matched nothing produces. Its blast radius today is nil —
// `bytes_copy_string` is not in `src/lib.npk` — which is exactly what was said
// about the use-after-free the first audit blocked on.
mod:bytes_copy_string_empty;

use "../../src/core/bytes.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    // The ONE difference from the sibling: nothing is pushed, so `b.len` is 0
    // and `string_concat("", "")` takes the path that has no short-circuit.
    Bytes:b = raw bytes_init(16i64);

    int64:k = 0i64;
    while (k < 8000000i64) decreases 8000000i64 - k {
        string:s = raw bytes_copy_string(@b);
        discard(s);
        k = k + 1i64;
    }

    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (StackExhausted) { exit 106i32; },
        (MachineFault) { exit 107i32; },
        (DecreasesViolated) { exit 108i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
